Chapter: PrimText
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The text and CCE tier of the primitive-cost table, and a regression test for
 two findings that live here. One shape per line, small enough that the
 expected number can be worked out on paper before the program runs.

 Bare metal's rules, so the numbers can be checked rather than trusted. A
 text is one block of `8 + align8(len)` -- a length word then the bytes
 (`emit-str-concat-slow-alloc`, Emit/X86_64TextHelpers.codex:223). An append
 onto an accumulator costs only the appended bytes, because bare metal
 recognises a tail-recursive accumulator parameter and calls
 `__str_concat_inplace` (`is-inplace-append`, Emit/X86_64.codex:2376). So a
 loop appending one byte n times costs `8 + align8(n)`, not `n(n+1)/2`.

 Finding 22 is the asymptotic one: the zig plug had no equivalent of that
 in-place path and always emitted a fresh concat, so the same loop cost
 exactly the triangular numbers -- 2080 / 8256 / 32896 at n = 64 / 128 / 256
 against bare metal's 72 / 136 / 264. The accumulator section below is that
 finding as a standing test: it prints LINEAR or QUADRATIC and says which.

 Finding 23 is the encoding one. A codepoint no CCE tier covers becomes `?`,
 CCE 68, silently -- that is upstream's choice and it is observable, so the
 plug has to make the same substitution rather than refuse. The character
 codes below assert it on both arms. U+22A2 occurs exactly once in the whole
 depot, in type-theory prose, and refusing it aborted the native loop five
 seconds into a 2.5 MB subject while bare metal compiled the same file.

 A char is a codepoint in the zig plug and a CCE code in the C# plug, and
 `char-code-at` is the one that answers in CCE. The codes below are CCE, so
 they are the same number on every arm or one of the arms is wrong.

 SUBSTRING AND SPLIT cost what a text of that length costs, on both arms.
 Bare metal copies: `emit-substring-alloc` bumps the frontier by
 `(len + 15) & ~7` and `emit-substring-copy` moves the bytes one at a time
 (Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:610 and :618). So does the plug, since finding 29
 -- a substring that allocates nothing cannot be placed on the deck, and a
 value that looks decked and is not is a table full of reclaimed bytes.

 What is left is the representation gap and nothing else: bare metal pays a
 length word and rounds to eight, we pay the bytes. A flat twelve on a
 four-byte piece. The scan is the shape that matters, because real subjects
 tokenize rather than take one substring -- 28 four-byte pieces out of a
 32-byte literal, 448 against 112.

 In-range only, deliberately. Bare metal traps on an out-of-range substring,
 at Damian's ruling that a clamp turns a program's bug into quietly wrong data
 (Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex:640-666), and so does the plug since finding 28.
 The out-of-range case has its own probe: a file that asserted it here would
 take these forty assertions down with it.

 measure rewinds after reading, so each line is independent and only the
 Integer escapes the window.

Section: Instrument

  measure : (Integer -> Integer) -> Integer
  measure (f) =
   let h0 = __heap-save
   in let r = f 0
   in let h1 = __heap-save
   in let back = __heap-restore h0
   in if r >= 0 then h1 - h0 else 0 - 1

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  ratio-text : Integer, Integer -> Text
  ratio-text (prev) (cur) =
   if prev <= 0 then "n/a"
   else let t = (cur * 10 + prev / 2) / prev
   in integer-to-text (t / 10) & "." & integer-to-text (t - (t / 10) * 10)

  verdict : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  verdict (a) (b) (c) =
   if a <= 0 then "UNMEASURED"
   else let r1 = (b * 10 + a / 2) / a
   in let r2 = (c * 10 + b / 2) / b
   in if (r1 >= 30) & (r2 >= 30) then "QUADRATIC"
   else if (r1 < 30) & (r2 < 30) then "LINEAR"
   else "MIXED"

Section: Shapes

  text-loop : Integer, Text -> Text
  text-loop (n) (acc) =
   if n <= 0 then acc else text-loop (n - 1) (acc & "x")

  cat-pair : Text, Text -> Integer
  cat-pair (a) (b) = text-length (a & b)

  sub-len : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sub-len (s) (a) (n) = text-length (substring s a n)

  split-count : Text, Text -> Integer
  split-count (s) (sep) = list-length (text-split s sep)

 Tokenizing, in miniature. The literal is static on both arms so it costs
 nothing; every byte measured is the pieces. Written with the source built
 inside the shape rather than captured, so no closure capture lands in the
 number.

  sub-loop : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sub-loop (s) (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then 0
   else let piece = substring s i 4
   in sub-loop s (i + 1) n

  sub-scan : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sub-scan (start) (n) =
   let src = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz012345"
   in sub-loop src start n

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("frontier observes allocation: " & show-yn (measure (\z -> text-length ("abcd" & "efgh")) > 0))
   print-line-uni ("meter reads a 1024 reserve  : " & show-yn (measure (\z -> let a = __heap-advance (z + 1024) in z) == 1024))
   print-line-uni "-- CCE codes: identical on every arm, or an arm is wrong --"
   print-line-uni ("code A        " & integer-to-text (char-code-at "A" 0))
   print-line-uni ("code z        " & integer-to-text (char-code-at "z" 0))
   print-line-uni ("code space    " & integer-to-text (char-code-at " " 0))
   print-line-uni ("code question " & integer-to-text (char-code-at "?" 0))
   print-line-uni ("code Gamma    " & integer-to-text (char-code-at "Γ" 0))
   print-line-uni ("code tau      " & integer-to-text (char-code-at "τ" 0))
   print-line-uni ("code eacute   " & integer-to-text (char-code-at "é" 0))
   print-line-uni "-- finding 23: an uncovered codepoint substitutes, it does not refuse --"
   print-line-uni ("turnstile code" & integer-to-text (char-code-at "⊢" 0))
   print-line-uni ("substitutes   " & show-yn (char-code-at "⊢" 0 == char-code-at "?" 0))
   print-line-uni ("length is one " & show-yn (text-length "⊢" == 1))
   print-line-uni "-- lengths --"
   print-line-uni ("len abcd      " & integer-to-text (text-length "abcd"))
   print-line-uni ("len empty     " & integer-to-text (text-length ""))
   print-line-uni ("len concat    " & integer-to-text (cat-pair "abcd" "efgh"))
   print-line-uni "-- cost: expect 8 + align8(len) --"
   print-line-uni ("cat 4 & 4     " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> cat-pair "abcd" "efgh")))
   print-line-uni ("cat 8 & 8     " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> cat-pair "abcdefgh" "ijklmnop")))
   print-line-uni ("cat 1 & 1     " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> cat-pair "a" "b")))
   print-line-uni ("cat empty     " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> cat-pair "" "abcd")))
   print-line-uni "-- substring semantics: identical on both arms, in range --"
   print-line-uni ("sub content   " & show-yn (text-compare (substring "abcdefgh" 2 3) "cde" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("sub from zero " & show-yn (text-compare (substring "abcdefgh" 0 4) "abcd" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("sub to the end" & show-yn (text-compare (substring "abcdefgh" 5 3) "fgh" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("sub of length0" & show-yn (text-length (substring "abcd" 0 0) == 0))
   print-line-uni ("sub at the end" & show-yn (text-length (substring "abcd" 4 0) == 0))
   print-line-uni ("sub whole text" & show-yn (text-compare (substring "abcd" 0 4) "abcd" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("sub of a built" & show-yn (text-compare (substring ("ab" & "cd") 1 2) "bc" == 0))
   print-line-uni "-- cost: both copy; bare metal 8 + align8(len), the plug the bytes --"
   print-line-uni ("sub 4 of 8    " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> sub-len "abcdefgh" z 4)))
   print-line-uni ("sub 8 of 8    " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> sub-len "abcdefgh" z 8)))
   print-line-uni ("sub 20 of 20  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> sub-len "abcdefghijklmnopqrst" z 20)))
   print-line-uni ("sub 0 of 4    " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> sub-len "abcd" z 0)))
   print-line-uni ("scan 28 x 4   " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> sub-scan z 28)))
   print-line-uni "-- split: pieces are copies on both arms; the list growth differs --"
   print-line-uni ("split abc     " & integer-to-text (split-count "a,b,c" ","))
   print-line-uni ("split none    " & integer-to-text (split-count "abc" ","))
   print-line-uni ("split empty   " & integer-to-text (split-count "" ","))
   print-line-uni ("split gap     " & integer-to-text (split-count "a,,b" ","))
   print-line-uni ("split trailing" & integer-to-text (split-count "a,b," ","))
   print-line-uni ("piece 1       " & show-yn (text-compare (list-at (text-split "a,bb,ccc" ",") 1) "bb" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("piece 2       " & show-yn (text-compare (list-at (text-split "a,bb,ccc" ",") 2) "ccc" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("split rejoins " & show-yn (text-compare (text-concat-list (text-split "a,bb,ccc" ",")) "abbccc" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("replace works " & show-yn (text-compare (text-replace "aXbXc" "X" "-") "a-b-c" == 0))
   print-line-uni ("cost split 3  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> split-count "a,b,c" ",")))
   print-line-uni ("cost split 6  " & integer-to-text (measure (\z -> split-count "aa,bb,cc,dd,ee,ff" ",")))
   print-line-uni "-- finding 22: accumulate one byte at a time, n / 2n / 4n --"
   let t1 = measure (\z -> text-length (text-loop (z + 64) ""))
   in let t2 = measure (\z -> text-length (text-loop (z + 128) ""))
   in let t3 = measure (\z -> text-length (text-loop (z + 256) ""))
   in act
     print-line-uni ("text-accum " & integer-to-text t1 & " " & integer-to-text t2 & " " & integer-to-text t3
       & "  ratios " & ratio-text t1 t2 & " " & ratio-text t2 t3 & " -> " & verdict t1 t2 t3)
   end
  end
